# ---- accepted exposure, no advisory id (#676; follow-up #775) ----
# The on-policy rollout server is vLLM's own OpenAI-compatible server: trl 1.x's
# `trl vllm-serve` (run_vllm_serve.py) execs `vllm serve` with VLLM_SERVER_DEV_MODE=1 and
# no --api-key. Two steps start one, and for the job's lifetime its routes are reachable,
# unauthenticated, from the cluster network, including by other users' jobs:
# - student-rollout-generator binds the node's advertised address (`--host "$ADDR"`,
#   student-rollout-generator/skypilot/step-template.yaml): the trainer that calls it runs
#   in a different allocation, so that address has to be reachable from the cluster.
# - logit-distill's in-allocation server (vllm_num_servers > 0, no `vllm` input; the
#   run_vllm_serve call in logit-distill/skypilot/step-template.yaml) passes no --host, so
#   it binds trl's default, 0.0.0.0: every interface of the serving node.
# A caller can generate with the student (/v1/completions, /v1/chat/completions,
# /tokenize), push weights into it (/init_weight_transfer_engine, /update_weights: the
# served model is then the caller's), call into the workers (/collective_rpc), pause, sleep
# or reset it, and with --enable-scale-out (trl passes it on vLLM >= 0.30) use
# /inference/v1/generate.
# --api-key would not close this: vLLM guards only /v1, /v2, /inference and /cohere paths,
# and trl's client sends no key. Accepted for now: the server holds only the student this
# build trained, and the exposure ends with the job. No vLLM 0.33.0 advisory remains
# below; re-judge any future one against this server. #775 is the
# follow-up: bind each server to the node's address, or to loopback where the trainer
# shares its node. Until 2027-05-31.

# ---- accepted HIGH advisories (tracked in #752) ----
# The vllm 0.33.0 lock (#676) has one HIGH advisory in osv, filelock's below: vllm, torch,
# transformers, accelerate, fsspec and setuptools all moved to fixed releases, and ray is
# no longer installed. The rest is what only trivy sees, inside a binary.

# filelock 3.32.7: the fix is 4.2.1, a major-version move. filelock 4.x changes
# UnixFileLock's behaviour on shared lock directories (4.2.12 refuses, with
# PermissionError, a lock file another account owns under a sticky parent), and these
# steps share one HF cache across accounts on group-shared filesystems. That move belongs
# in its own change, tested on the cluster (#752); pyproject.toml holds it with a
# filelock<4 constraint (vllm 0.34.0 would force it). Not reachable: the flaw is only in
# ReadWriteLock / AsyncReadWriteLock (the SQLite-backed lock, added in 3.20.4), and no
# module in the venv outside filelock itself names ReadWriteLock (checked in the vllm
# 0.33.0 / torch 2.14.0 dev image), nor does the distill code. Until 2027-05-31.
CVE-2026-112940
GHSA-v4x8-3wr5-3wq8

